\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Does the linear-programming auxiliary-function method prove universal optimality for a finite spherical configuration exactly when the linear-programming bound for spherical codes is sharp for that configuration; and does its Euclidean analogue prove universal optimality for the hexagonal lattice, the $E_8$ root lattice, and the Leech lattice?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2005/17}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does the linear-programming auxiliary-function method prove universal optimality for a finite spherical configuration exactly when the linear-programming bound for spherical codes is sharp for that configuration; and does its Euclidean analogue prove universal optimality for the hexagonal lattice, the $E_8$ root lattice, and the Leech lattice?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 30000212 (OWR-822-002). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2005/17}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:30000212}

